From 02992ba41d9cc13dd1a7abf442dac8daba9b81dc Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ebg-ant <296364998+ebg-ant@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 15 Oct 2026 12:29:35 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] fix: one step of Undo gives a window that was fetched from
 the edge its place back
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

A floating window that hangs over an edge and is asked for by name (a row of Windows in the menu, Go to…, find, reveal)
comes in whole. Arbor moved a window at a person's word, and ⌃⌥Z said "nothing to undo": where a window floats is no part
of an arrangement, and only a change to the arrangement was a step.

A step can now say whose place it is about (UndoStep.places; empty for every other step). Taking it back is the mirror of
the fetch: it moves that one window to what is remembered as its own place, and lays nothing out. The place at the edge
counts as left there by its person, so the layouts that follow leave it. In the Dock or hidden with its app it is moved where
nobody sees it; out of view since, the view steps out far enough to show it; moved by hand since, it stays where the hand put
it. While its app does not answer the step stays, and the reply says so. The menu says Undo Move, whichever way the window
was asked for, also when Arbor made room for a docked window on the way.

Nothing is offered that cannot be done. From where no hand could reach its title bar the window was fetched for good; one
that is kept in view, summoned or a guest has its place by that; where macOS or its app had moved it since its person last
did, the screen and what is remembered know no common place; and with floatingWindowsMakeWay on every layout places it anew.
After such a fetch ⌃⌥Z says once that the move cannot be undone and what the next press takes back, and moves nothing:
whoever has just seen a window move means that window, not the dock before it. The menu's row names what is older, as after
a close: a row names nothing that it will not do. With nothing older there is nothing to undo.

One refusal is for all that cannot be taken back: what lies under the one that is said and cannot be taken back either, or
has nothing left to show, goes with it. Two closes with such a step between them refused twice, and the first refusal promised
a press that was then refused; F19 says that the press after a refusal goes on with the older step.

A window whose app does not answer when it is asked for no longer comes in when the app wakes, when nobody is asking any
more, by a move no Undo knows of. Nothing comes of it, nothing is owed, nothing else is tried, and it is said: "Mail is not
answering: try again in a moment", in the reply and on screen. So that it is said in Go to… too, a row chosen there runs as a
command from one of Arbor's own windows, as side by side chosen there already does.

A window that fills the screen is whole by that and is no longer fetched, which moved it twice. That an undo has come to
nothing because an app does not answer is asked of the windows themselves: that the arrangement is as it was is also true of a
step that is about a place.

check-window-list.sh: a claim for this; and its check that no title is in the trace no longer trips over its own words, which
the harness notes in every recording since it records every session.
